  {
   "en": "So is this the passive?",
   "kind": "chrome",
   "where": "[How it's built] div.camp#form > div.chart-wrap > h3",
   "section": "form",
   "tr": {}
  },
  {
   "en": "Half of it is, which is why the causative sits on the descent side of the mountain rather than up with the tenses. The main clause is <strong>active</strong> — you are the subject and you did the arranging. What follows <em>have</em> is a <strong>passive</strong> in miniature: <em>my hair cut</em> is <em>my hair [was] cut</em> with the auxiliary squeezed out. Many coursebooks call it the <em>causative passive</em> for exactly that reason.",
   "kind": "chrome",
   "where": "[How it's built] div.camp#form > div.chart-wrap > p.chart-note",
   "section": "form",
   "tr": {}
  },
